{"title":"Improper fraction","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><p>An <b>improper fraction</b>, also called a <b>top-heavy fraction</b> in Britain, is a <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Fraction\" title=\"Fraction\">fraction</a> whose <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Absolute_value\" title=\"Absolute value\">absolute value</a> is greater than 1.  For example, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/c/8/3c81650f05194000234dbf06e20948e00fd69cec.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle \\frac 7 3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"11\" height=\"37\" /> is an improper fraction.  Fractions which are not improper are called <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Proper_fraction\" title=\"Proper fraction\">proper fractions</a>.\n</p><p>Improper fractions may be used as a counting system, and can also be used in cryptography. See <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Improper_fractional_base\" title=\"Improper fractional base\">improper fractional base</a>.\n</p><p><i>This article is a stub.  Help us out by <span class=\"plainlinks\"><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/index.php?title=Improper_fraction&amp;action=edit\">expanding it</a></span>.</i>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_also\">See also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Improper_fraction&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: See also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<ul><li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Fraction\" title=\"Fraction\">Fraction</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Encryption&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"Encryption (page does not exist)\">Encryption</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Base\" title=\"Base\">Base</a></li></ul>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261017230428\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced exp